{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792796625,"nonce":"lyN7FP1bEAa2mMe0He21H2vtmGg=","title":"Python: raytrace's short-lived vectors stay on the heap","message":"raytrace-simple is about 1.9x behind PyPy in CI. Sampled on the Mac (Cranelift, address-mapped), zyntax_alloc and zyntax_free are about half of the program thread, called from Sphere.intersectionTime, Scene._lightIsVisible, Scene.render, PpmCanvas.plot and Vector.scale. In intersectionTime, `cp = self.centre - ray.point` is a Vector used only for two dot products and freed on every path, yet it is allocated: scalar_replace_alloc keeps an object to one block, and heap_scalarize starts only from pointer phis and takes only scalar fields, while Vector's fields are number structs `{ i64, i64, f64 }` (an int-or-float field) stored and loaded whole.\n\nWhat is left after letting heap_scalarize start from allocations and take struct fields: _lightIsVisible's Ray and vectors escape into the dispatched o.intersectionTime(...) call, and Optional[float] returns are boxed (zb_box_f64, zb_num_box).","files":null}]}